# Shell scripts have to be LF, and not only in the committed blob.
#
# `test-orchestrator/runner/*.sh` is bind-mounted into the runner container and
# executed there by its shebang. On a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`
# the working tree gets CRLF, and `#!/usr/bin/env bash` becomes `bash\r` — the
# script dies with `No such file or directory` before its first command, while
# `bash -n` still calls it syntactically fine, because a comment with a stray CR
# is a comment. That cost a verification run whose failure looked like a
# conclusion about the code under test.
#
# `gradlew` is the same shape and is executed inside the Linux container too.
*.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf

# The protos are the same story with a different symptom. `buf format --diff
# --exit-code` is a CI gate, and on a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`
# every line of `observation.proto` reads as changed while the committed blob is
# already formatted — so the gate looks red locally, is green in CI, and the
# difference is nothing either of them can see. Pin the line endings so the answer
# is the same in both places.
*.proto text eol=lf
